Lab 5: Monitor and Maintain Control Plane Health
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Monitor control plane services using open-source tools (Prometheus, Grafana, and Alertmanager)
Perform a rolling upgrade of a control plane service (using Kubernetes as an example)
Troubleshoot common control plane issues (API server unavailability, etcd failures, and resource constraints)
Prerequisites
A Linux-based system (Ubuntu 20.04/22.04 recommended)
Basic understanding of Kubernetes/OpenShift control plane components
Administrative access to a Kubernetes cluster (Minikube, Kind, or OpenShift)
Docker/Podman installed (for container management)
kubectl or oc CLI installed
Helm installed (for deploying monitoring tools)
Task 1: Monitor Control Plane Services Using Open-Source Tools
Subtask 1.1: Deploy Prometheus and Grafana for Monitoring
Install Helm (if not already installed):

curl -fsSL -o get_helm.sh https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm/main/scripts/get-helm-3
chmod 700 get_helm.sh
./get_helm.sh
Add Prometheus Helm repository:

helm repo add prometheus-community https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts
helm repo update
Install Prometheus Stack (includes Grafana):

helm install prometheus-stack prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack -n monitoring --create-namespace
Verify installation:

kubectl get pods -n monitoring
Expected Output:
All pods should be in Running state.

Access Grafana Dashboard:

kubectl port-forward svc/prometheus-stack-grafana -n monitoring 3000:80
Open http://localhost:3000 in a browser.
Default credentials: admin/prom-operator.
Subtask 1.2: Configure Alerts for Control Plane Health
Check Prometheus Alerts:

kubectl port-forward svc/prometheus-stack-kube-prom-prometheus -n monitoring 9090:9090
Open http://localhost:9090/alerts to see pre-configured Kubernetes alerts.
Create a Custom Alert for API Server Downtime:

Edit prometheus-custom-rules.yaml:
groups:
- name: control-plane-health
  rules:
  - alert: KubeAPIDown
    expr: up{job="apiserver"} == 0
    for: 5m
    labels:
      severity: critical
    annotations:
      summary: "Kubernetes API server is down"
      description: "API server has been unreachable for 5 minutes."
Apply the rule:
kubectl create configmap custom-rules --from-file=prometheus-custom-rules.yaml -n monitoring
kubectl label configmap custom-rules app.kubernetes.io/name=kube-prometheus-stack -n monitoring
Task 2: Perform a Rolling Upgrade of a Control Plane Service (Kube-Proxy Example)
Subtask 2.1: Check Current Kube-Proxy Version
List DaemonSets (Kube-Proxy runs as a DaemonSet):

kubectl get daemonsets -n kube-system
Check current image version:

kubectl describe daemonset kube-proxy -n kube-system | grep Image
Subtask 2.2: Upgrade Kube-Proxy
Edit DaemonSet to use a newer version:

kubectl edit daemonset kube-proxy -n kube-system
Change image: k8s.gcr.io/kube-proxy:v1.XX.X to a newer version (e.g., v1.28.0).
Verify rolling update:

kubectl rollout status daemonset/kube-proxy -n kube-system
Expected Output:
daemon set "kube-proxy" successfully rolled out

Task 3: Troubleshoot Common Control Plane Issues
Subtask 3.1: Diagnose API Server Unavailability
Check API Server Logs:

kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-apiserver-<node-name>
Test API Server:

curl -k https://<API_SERVER_IP>:6443/healthz
Expected Output:
ok

Subtask 3.2: Resolve etcd Cluster Issues
Check etcd Health:

kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep etcd
kubectl exec -it etcd-<pod-name> -n kube-system -- etcdctl endpoint health
Fix etcd Quorum Loss (if needed):

kubectl exec -it etcd-<pod-name> -n kube-system -- etcdctl member list
Conclusion
In this lab, you learned how to:

Monitor control plane health using Prometheus, Grafana, and Alertmanager.
Perform a rolling upgrade of a control plane service (kube-proxy).
Troubleshoot common control plane issues (API server, etcd).
Next Steps
Explore OpenShift-specific monitoring with OpenShift Monitoring Stack.
Practice node-level troubleshooting with journalctl and crictl.
Troubleshooting Tips
If Grafana is not loading, check if the prometheus-stack-grafana service is running.
If kubectl commands fail, verify kubeconfig permissions.
For etcd issues, ensure odd-numbered cluster size for quorum.
